# Review: "Towards a Complete Logical Framework for GNN Expressiveness"

---

## Critical Issues

### 1. The Claimed Equivalence Is Not an Equivalence — It Is an Approximation in One Direction [Structural]

**Definition 2**, which introduces the paper's central concept of an "equivalent logic set," contains four bullets that are not symmetric. The second bullet states that every formula φ ∈ Φ is **exactly** captured by some GNN χ ∈ X on all graphs. But the fourth bullet states only that for any χ ∈ X and any positive integer N, there **exists** a φ ∈ Φ that agrees with χ on all graphs with **no more than N nodes**. This is a finite approximation property, not equivalence. A single GNN with fixed weights is a function on graphs of all sizes; the φ that approximates it on graphs of size ≤ N may fail to agree for larger graphs.

This asymmetry is foundational. If the GNN→logic direction is only approximate (agreeing up to N nodes for each N separately), then GNNs may be strictly more expressive than the stated logic fragment on infinite families of graphs. Every downstream result — Proposition 7, Corollary 10, Theorem 8 — inherits this caveat. Yet the paper consistently writes "equivalent logic set" and derives exact equalities in Corollary 10 (e.g., "MPNNs = 1-WL") without acknowledging that the equality is underpinned by this finite approximation. If the paper means something non-standard by "equivalent," it must show that the standard separating-pair sense of equivalence is recovered. It does not.

This is not a terminological quibble. A reader who takes Definition 2 seriously must conclude that the paper proves a logic fragment *approximates* GNN behavior for any fixed graph size, not that it *characterizes* GNN behavior. These are very different claims.

---

### 2. The "Complete" Framework Explicitly Excludes Large Classes of Modern GNNs [Structural]

The title says "complete logical framework" and the abstract says the framework applies to "arbitrary GNN architectures." But Section 7 explicitly excludes GNNs that inject distance or structural information beyond aggregation and combination (Graphormer-GD is the only example named). In practice, this exclusion is much broader: GNNs with spectral operations, attention mechanisms that depend on global graph structure, positional encoding layers, any model that uses precomputed graph-level features, and transformers over graphs all fail to decompose cleanly into the AGG/COM paradigm as defined. The paper does not give a criterion for determining whether a given architecture is or is not a GACNN, leaving the scope of "arbitrary GNN architectures" undefined.

Calling a framework "complete" when its applicability is restricted to one specific design paradigm is a category error. The mismatch between the abstract's universalist framing and the body's restricted scope is not a limitation to acknowledge — it invalidates the framing. A paper entitled "a complete logical framework" must cover all GNNs or renounce the word "complete."

---

### 3. The Strict Inequalities in Corollary 10 Are Not Established [Evidential]

Corollary 10 states: MPNNs = 1-WL < Subgraph GNNs (weak) = NBFNet < Subgraph GNNs (strong) < Local 2-FGNN < FGNN = 3-WL, 1-WL < SEAL < 4-WL.

Proposition 9 provides a method for deriving **upper** bounds (i.e., X ≤ k-WL) by counting variables. The paper's framework, via Proposition 5 and Theorem 3, derives which logic fragments are in Φ, which characterizes an upper bound. But the **lower bounds** — i.e., that each model truly achieves the stated expressivity rather than merely being bounded by it — require separate arguments: either exhibiting pairs of graphs the model separates but a weaker model cannot, or showing the logic fragment is realizable. Nowhere in the main text are such separation witnesses given for the strict inequalities. The paper mentions (Section 6.3) only the variable-counting direction. For example, the claim "FGNN = 3-WL" requires showing 2-FGNNs are **exactly** as expressive as 3-WL, not merely that they are bounded by it. Without this, Corollary 10 presents upper-bound results as exact equalities.

---

## Section-by-Section Notes

**Abstract / Introduction.** The claim that the framework "not only explains existing models, but also provides inspiration for future research" is vague and unsubstantiated. More concretely, the abstract says the paper presents "a general method for determining the homomorphism expressivity of arbitrary GNN models" but Theorem 8 requires the neighbor relation to be "described by composition of edges" and assumes no node/edge labels — restrictions stated only in the body of Section 6.2, not here.

**Section 3.1 (Definition 2).** As noted above, the second and fourth bullets are in tension. Additionally, the fifth bullet ("cannot distinguish u, v iff all logic formulas classify u, v the same") is stated without justification. This is itself an equivalence claim that needs proof or citation. Without it, the definition does not clearly establish that Φ is a well-defined object at all.

**Section 5.1 (Theorem 3).** The theorem is stated without a proof sketch in the main text, and the proof is in the appendix. Given that this is the central result on which the entire paper depends, the absence of any intuition for **why** the logic sets are provably complete (not just sound) is a significant gap. The remark following Theorem 3 explains the sound direction (GNNs ↔ logical operations) well, but the completeness direction is silent. This is fine for a conference venue if the appendix proof is rigorous, but the main text provides no assurance.

**Proposition 7.** The formula for 2-GNN reads: φ(x,y) := ∃^{≥N} z (φ'(x,z) | ∃^{≥N} z (φ'(z,y))). The variable z is reused inside the nested quantifier, which the paper explicitly says to avoid (footnote 1 in Section 6.2). This appears to be an error or inconsistency within the same paper's conventions.

**Section 6.2 (Homomorphism Expressivity).** The procedure for constructing ℱ from Φ is restricted to the label-free case and to neighbor relations defined as edge compositions. The example of 2-FGNNs is instructive, but Theorem 8 is stated without these assumptions prominently, creating the impression of greater generality than is delivered.

**Section 6.3.** The argument that counting variables in the grammar suffices for determining k-WL bounds (Proposition 9) is stated as a trivial consequence of Proposition 5 and Cai et al. (1992), but it is not trivial. Variable reuse (as noted in footnote 1) complicates counting. If two nested existential quantifiers reuse a variable, the effective variable count is lower than naive counting would suggest. The paper does not address whether the grammars in Proposition 5 can always be written in variable-distinct form without increasing the variable count.

**Section 7 (Limitations).** The limitation statement is one paragraph and too brief. It does not acknowledge the finite-approximation issue in Definition 2, nor the strict-inequality gap in Corollary 10, nor the label-free restriction in Theorem 8.

