## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)

1. **General, constructive framework for logical expressivity of arbitrary GNNs**: Theorem 3 and Proposition 5 provide a recursive construction that maps any GACNN computation graph—including models with heterogeneous aggregation and combination operations—to an equivalent logic set in FOC. Unlike prior work (Barceló et al., 2020), which only characterized MPNNs, this framework applies to any GNN decomposable into AGG/COM operations, enabling analysis of novel architectures without bespoke proofs. The construction is shown in Eq. (3) of Theorem 3 and the layer-agnostic version in Proposition 5.

2. **Unified logical characterization of 8+ prominent GNN architectures**: Proposition 7 gives explicit logical grammars for MPNN, Subgraph GNN (weak and strong), NBFNet, Local 2-GNN, 2-FGNN, SEAL, and 2-GNN, all derived from the same GACNN framework. This unifies previously separate lines of analysis (node-level, subgraph-based, and link-prediction GNNs) and enables direct, interpretable comparison. For example, the characterization explains why 2-GNN is unsuitable for link prediction (it cannot express GrandParent), while NBFNet can.

3. **General method for homomorphism expressivity of arbitrary GACNNs, resolving an open problem**: Theorem 8 and the preceding construction procedure (Steps 1–2) provide a general recipe for determining the homomorphism expressivity of any GACNN model from its equivalent logic set, extending the methods of Dell et al. (2018) and Zhang et al. (2024). The paper explicitly states this resolves a conjecture from Zhang et al. (2024) regarding when a GNN's homomorphism expressivity exists (Section 6.2).

### Supporting strengths

1. **Simple method for deriving WL upper bounds from logic**: Proposition 9 shows that a GNN's expressivity is bounded by k-WL iff its equivalent logic formulas use at most k variables. Since the logic grammars in Proposition 5 make variable counts trivially inspectable, this eliminates the need for explicit WL hierarchy constructions for new models.

2. **Complete expressivity hierarchy for popular GNNs**: Corollary 10 establishes the ordering MPNNs = 1-WL < Subgraph GNNs (weak) = NBFNet < Subgraph GNNs (strong) < Local 2-FGNN < FGNN = 3-WL, and 1-WL < SEAL < 4-WL—all derived from the logical framework rather than ad hoc graph-pair constructions.

### Presentation / practical strengths

1. **Concrete worked examples**: The 2-FGNN homomorphism expressivity construction (Section 6.2, Figure 2f) explicitly walks through the recursive procedure, starting from F⁽⁰⁾ and building up, making the general method accessible. Similarly, Figure 1 clearly illustrates the correspondence between GACNN computation graphs and logic set hierarchies.

## Summary

This paper introduces a unified framework for determining the logical expressivity of arbitrary GNNs by decomposing them into General Aggregate-Combine Neural Networks (GACNNs) and recursively constructing equivalent logic sets in first-order logic with counting quantifiers. The single most important piece of evidence is Theorem 3 (and its layer-agnostic version, Proposition 5), which provides the constructive mapping from any GACNN computation graph to its equivalent logic set; this is validated by successfully deriving explicit logical characterizations for 8+ prominent GNN architectures (Proposition 7), establishing a complete expressivity hierarchy (Corollary 10), and providing a general method for homomorphism expressivity (Theorem 8).

